 "title": "Percentages & Compound Change",
 "tier": "both",
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Identify the percentage and whether it is an increase or decrease",
   "Calculate the multiplier: increase → (1 + r/100), decrease → (1 − r/100)",
   "Multiply the original amount by the multiplier",
   "For compound change, raise the multiplier to the power n",
   "For reverse percentages, divide the final amount by the multiplier"
  ],
  "title": "How to Work with Percentages & Compound Change",
  "content": "<p>For a percentage <strong>increase</strong>, multiply by \\(1 + \\frac{\\%}{100}\\). For a <strong>decrease</strong>, multiply by \\(1 - \\frac{\\%}{100}\\). These are called <strong>multipliers</strong>.</p><p><strong>Compound change</strong> means applying the same percentage repeatedly. After \\(n\\) years at rate \\(r\\%\\):</p><p>$$\\text{Final} = \\text{Original} \\times \\left(1 \\pm \\frac{r}{100}\\right)^n$$</p><p>For <strong>reverse percentages</strong>, divide by the multiplier to find the original amount. If a price after a 20% increase is £180, the original was \\(\\frac{180}{1.20} = £150\\).</p><p>Common mistake: for reverse percentages, do NOT find 20% of the new price and subtract — the 20% was applied to the original, unknown amount.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>A car worth £12,000 depreciates by 15% per year. What is it worth after 3 years?</strong></p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Multiplier = \\(1 - 0.15 = 0.85\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Value = \\(12\\,000 \\times 0.85^3\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> \\(= 12\\,000 \\times 0.614125 = £7\\,369.50\\)</p>"
 },
